\section{The kernel chooses the first user instruction}

Levine's basic loader creates the program's address space, supplies startup information, and starts execution \cite[chapter~8]{levine2000linkers}. For a dynamically linked ELF executable he adds a second image: the interpreter, usually the runtime dynamic linker. The system maps both images and begins in the interpreter, not in the executable \cite[chapter~10]{levine2000linkers}. On current Linux, the interpreter pathname comes from the file range described by the \texttt{PT\_INTERP} program header. Link editors commonly label those bytes \texttt{.interp}, but the kernel does not look up that section by name. A conventional executable with no interpreter instead begins at its own runtime entry. Having ELF type \texttt{ET\_DYN} alone does not settle the question: an ordinary dynamic PIE has an interpreter, while a static PIE can start without one and handle its own startup relocations.

An ELF \texttt{e\_entry} is a link-time virtual address, not a file offset and not necessarily a language function address. A PIE and its interpreter can have different load biases. Add the executable's bias to its own entry and the interpreter's bias to the interpreter's entry. Neither calculation should use the base of the stack. A single instruction pointer selects the first instruction; separate startup data tells that code where control must go later.

\section{The initial stack is an ABI record}

On System V AMD64 the incoming stack pointer is 16-byte aligned, with the argument count at the address it points to. The argument pointer array follows and ends in a null pointer. Then come the environment pointers and their null terminator, followed by the auxiliary vector. Its entries are type/value pairs, terminated by a pair whose type is \texttt{AT\_NULL}. The layout and tag meanings are described in \S3.4 of the AMD64 ABI supplement. The two null pointers and the auxiliary vector's terminating tag serve different roles; a zero value attached to a different tag is not the end of the vector.

For the Linux handoff, \texttt{AT\_PHDR} gives a runtime address for the executable's program headers, with \texttt{AT\_PHENT} the size of an entry and \texttt{AT\_PHNUM} the count. \texttt{AT\_BASE} describes the interpreter's load base and \texttt{AT\_ENTRY} gives the executable's already adjusted entry address. The AMD64 ABI also describes an alternate \texttt{AT\_EXECFD} interface, in which the interpreter receives a file descriptor instead of an in-memory program header location. It is not the ordinary Linux ELF handoff described here.

