\section{Trace the two entry addresses}

Take a hypothetical 64-bit Linux process with two arguments and one environment string. Assume the main program header table starts at image virtual address \texttt{0x40}, as it would if file offset \texttt{0x40} lay in a load segment starting at file offset and link address zero. The stack and address calculations below are independent; all machine words in the two pointer vectors take eight bytes.

\begin{verbatim}
argv = sp + 8; require argv[argc] == NULL
envp = argv + 8 * (argc + 1)    # skip the argv terminator
auxv = word_after_env_null(envp)
tags = scan_pairs_until_AT_NULL(auxv)
first_pc = interp_bias + interp_e_entry
next_pc = tags[AT_ENTRY]        # already a runtime address
\end{verbatim}

Here the argument pointers occupy stack offsets \texttt{0x08} and \texttt{0x10}, the argument terminator is at \texttt{0x18}, and the environment pointer and terminator are at \texttt{0x20} and \texttt{0x28}. The first auxiliary pair starts at \texttt{0x30}. With main bias \texttt{0x55555000} and entry \texttt{0x1060}, expect \texttt{AT\_ENTRY=0x55556060} and \texttt{AT\_PHDR=0x55555040}. Thirteen entries of 56 bytes cover \texttt{0x2d8} bytes, ending at \texttt{0x55555318}, exclusive. With interpreter bias \texttt{0x7f200000} and entry \texttt{0x1a20}, the kernel's first user instruction is at \texttt{0x7f201a20}. Confusing the two addresses would either skip initialization or reenter the interpreter.

\Cref{fig:visual-292} locates the auxiliary vector without treating its offset as fixed and separates the two entry addresses.

