Test the invariant that the application entry lies in the application's executable load range and the first instruction lies in the interpreter's executable load range, using the respective biases. In the stack trace, change the environment to empty. The environment terminator moves to \texttt{0x20} and the auxiliary vector to \texttt{0x28}; a parser using the previous constant offset now reads the first pair incorrectly.

\section{Finish bootstrap before entering the language runtime}

The interpreter starts without the benefit of an already initialized dynamic linker. Its earliest code is arranged to find its own dynamic information and perform the necessary self-relocations before relying on ordinary relocated references. Levine gives one historical implementation; reserved slots and the bootstrap instruction sequence are ABI- and implementation-specific, not rules for reading the initial stack. The interpreter then discovers dependencies, relocates the loaded images, and participates in startup initialization before passing control to the executable's entry. Some initialization is left to executable-side runtime startup; do not infer the whole constructor schedule from the transfer address.

The executable entry is normally a startup routine such as \texttt{\_start}. It mediates between the stack record and the normal language calling convention; the kernel has not called \texttt{main} with C arguments in registers. To inspect a local PIE, compare the linked entry from \texttt{readelf -hW} and interpreter from \texttt{readelf -lW} with the live value of \texttt{getauxval(AT\_ENTRY)} printed by that PIE. Subtracting the linked entry from the live address gives the main image's bias, not \texttt{AT\_BASE}.
